{
  "title": "Mirror-Image Orchard",
  "description": "## Task\n\nAn orchard is planted as a binary tree. It is a mirror-image orchard when the left half of the tree is the mirror image of the right half: swapping left and right everywhere in the root's left subtree gives exactly the right subtree, including the values.\n\n## Input\n\n- `level_order` — the tree in level order. The tree is given in level order as a list: the first entry is the root, then the children of each present node follow in order, left child then right child, with `null` marking a missing child. Missing children of missing nodes are not listed, and trailing `null` entries may be omitted. An empty list is an empty tree. Values are integers.\n\n## Output\n\n`True` if the tree is a mirror image of itself, otherwise `False`. An empty tree and a single node both count as mirror images.\n\n## Example\n\n```python\nis_mirror_orchard([1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4, 3])  # returns True\nis_mirror_orchard([1, 2, 2, None, 3, None, 3])  # returns False\nis_mirror_orchard([])  # returns True\n```\n\n## Constraints\n\n- 0 ≤ length of `level_order` ≤ 10^4\n- Node values are integers between -1000 and 1000.\n",
  "signature": "def is_mirror_orchard(level_order: list[int | None]) -> bool",
  "topic": "binary tree",
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "category": "graphs-trees",
